::ENG1_U04_L07_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.W.03] Prompt: Source finding: Some students with online calendar access missed fewer due dates. Which sentence is publication-ready?
{
~A. Online calendars make every student perfect. #Incorrect. This exaggerates the finding and is not accurate.
=B. A student survey suggests that online calendar access may help some students miss fewer due dates. #Correct. This is accurate, cautious, and source-based.
~C. Calendars are amazing and fix everything. #Incorrect. This is informal and unsupported.
~D. Dates and students are school things. #Incorrect. This is vague and not source-based.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Audience: families. Topic: online assignment calendars. Which heading fits best?
{
~A. Calendar Stuff. #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. A History of Every Calendar. #Incorrect. This is too broad for the publication purpose.
=C. How Online Calendars Help Students Track Due Dates. #Correct. This heading is clear and useful for families.
~D. Things to Know Maybe. #Incorrect. This heading does not guide the audience.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Which source wording is accurate for publication if a survey says "some students reported fewer distractions"?
{
~A. All students had no distractions ever. #Incorrect. This exaggerates the source.
~B. Quiet rooms definitely solve homework for everyone. #Incorrect. This changes the source meaning.
~C. Nobody needs quiet spaces. #Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
=D. Some students reported fewer distractions when using quiet study spaces. #Correct. This preserves the source meaning.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Which checklist item should come before publication?
{
~A. Make the title colorful before checking facts. #Incorrect. Appearance should not replace accuracy checks.
~B. Remove all source references. #Incorrect. Source-based communication should keep research clear.
=C. Verify that each source finding is accurate and connected to the purpose. #Correct. Accuracy and purpose are essential before publication.
~D. Add random facts to fill space. #Incorrect. Random facts weaken publication quality.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Prompt: Which sentence uses standard English?
{
~A. The source suggest quiet rooms helps students. #Incorrect. The verbs do not agree with the subjects.
=B. The source suggests that quiet study access may reduce homework distractions. #Correct. This sentence is clear and grammatically correct.
~C. Quiet rooms helping students homework. #Incorrect. This is not a complete standard sentence.
~D. Source and rooms and distractions. #Incorrect. This is a fragment list.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Which product is most publication-ready for students?
{
=A. A revised digital summary with headings, accurate findings, and clear tone. #Correct. This product is organized and audience-ready.
~B. A page of copied notes with no headings. #Incorrect. Copied notes and no headings are not ready for readers.
~C. A private draft with no audience. #Incorrect. Publication requires an audience.
~D. A random list of unrelated source facts. #Incorrect. Publication-ready work needs organization and relevance.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.W.03] Prompt: Which sentence best integrates source information?
{
~A. The source is a source about stuff. #Incorrect. This is vague and not informative.
=B. A school report found that students used the media center most before first period. #Correct. This introduces source information clearly.
~C. Students, report, media center. #Incorrect. This is a fragment list.
~D. It proves the media center is always best. #Incorrect. This exaggerates the source finding.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Audience: ninth-grade students. Which tone is most appropriate?
{
~A. This thing is totally awesome and everyone must agree. #Incorrect. This is informal and forceful.
~B. Only foolish students ignore this. #Incorrect. This is insulting and inappropriate.
~C. Whatever, read it if you want. #Incorrect. This tone is dismissive.
=D. This summary explains how quiet study spaces may help students complete homework with fewer distractions. #Correct. This is clear, respectful, and audience-appropriate.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.V.01] Prompt: Which academic phrase best introduces a cautious source-based conclusion?
{
~A. Everybody knows #Incorrect. This is too broad and unsupported.
~B. It is obvious forever #Incorrect. This is exaggerated and not academic.
=C. The findings suggest #Correct. This phrase is academic and avoids overstating the evidence.
~D. Stuff proves #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Which role best supports publication readiness?
{
=A. Source checker. #Correct. A source checker verifies that research findings are accurate.
~B. Topic changer. #Incorrect. Changing the topic disrupts the publication plan.
~C. Evidence remover. #Incorrect. Removing evidence weakens source-based communication.
~D. Confusion maker. #Incorrect. This role would harm the product.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q11::[MLA.ENG1.W.03] Prompt: Which revision improves source accuracy? Draft: "Tutoring always makes every student confident."
{
~A. Tutoring fixes every school problem. #Incorrect. This is exaggerated and unsupported.
~B. Tutoring is perfect for everybody. #Incorrect. This is absolute and inaccurate.
~C. Students never need help after tutoring. #Incorrect. This adds unsupported meaning.
=D. A student survey suggests that tutoring may help some students feel more confident. #Correct. This revision avoids exaggeration and keeps the source-based claim cautious.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q12::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Which heading best fits a publication about peer tutoring and confidence?
{
~A. Random Tutoring Things. #Incorrect. This heading is vague because it does not tell readers the specific topic or purpose.
=B. How Peer Tutoring Can Build Student Confidence. #Correct. This heading is clear, focused, and audience-friendly.
~C. Stuff About Students. #Incorrect. This does not identify the focus of peer tutoring or student confidence.
~D. Every Fact About School. #Incorrect. This is too broad because the publication has a specific tutoring focus.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q13::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Why must source-based publications avoid exaggeration?
{
=A. Exaggeration can change the source meaning and mislead readers. #Correct. Publication-ready work must protect source accuracy.
~B. Exaggeration always makes sources more reliable. #Incorrect. Exaggeration weakens reliability.
~C. Exaggeration replaces evidence. #Incorrect. Evidence should not be replaced by overstatement.
~D. Exaggeration helps readers ignore the source. #Incorrect. Readers need accurate source information.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q14::[MLA.ENG1.W.03] Prompt: Which sentence is best for a publication-ready summary?
{
~A. The source says good stuff about gardens. #Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. Gardens are cool and plants are green. #Incorrect. This is informal and not source-based.
=C. A science class report found that garden activities helped students connect vocabulary to plant observations. #Correct. This is specific, source-based, and clear.
~D. Vocabulary is a thing in school. #Incorrect. This is too general.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q15::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Which product problem should be fixed before publication?
{
~A. The heading clearly names the topic. #Incorrect. A clear heading is a strength.
~B. Source findings are accurate. #Incorrect. Accurate findings are a strength.
~C. The tone is formal and respectful. #Incorrect. Formal tone is appropriate.
=D. The product has no audience or purpose. #Correct. Audience and purpose must be clear before publication.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q16::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Prompt: Which sentence is ready for publication?
{
~A. The report explain students uses calendars. #Incorrect. Grammar errors make it unready.
=B. The report explains that online calendars may help students track due dates. #Correct. The sentence is grammatical and clear.
~C. Online calendars helps students tracking dates. #Incorrect. The sentence has agreement and wording errors.
~D. Calendar students due dates source. #Incorrect. This is not a complete sentence.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q17::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Which publication choice best supports scanning?
{
~A. One long paragraph with no headings. #Incorrect. This makes scanning difficult.
~B. Hidden source findings. #Incorrect. Readers need clear source-based information.
~C. Random order with no labels. #Incorrect. This harms readability.
=D. Clear headings and short source-based sections. #Correct. These features help readers find information quickly.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q18::[MLA.ENG1.W.03] Prompt: Which sentence best connects evidence to purpose?
{
~A. This source is about things. #Incorrect. This is too vague because it does not name the evidence or explain its purpose.
=B. The survey finding supports the summary's purpose by showing how quiet study spaces may reduce distractions. #Correct. This explains how evidence supports the communication goal.
~C. The heading is blue. #Incorrect. This does not connect evidence to purpose.
~D. The source exists. #Incorrect. This does not explain how the evidence supports the purpose of the product.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q19::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Audience: school leaders. Which publication choice best fits?
{
=A. Concise evidence summary with a clear recommendation. #Correct. This fits the audience and purpose.
~B. Slang-filled poster with no evidence. #Incorrect. School leaders need clear evidence and formal tone.
~C. Private notes with no final product. #Incorrect. That is not publication-ready.
~D. Random images with no explanation. #Incorrect. This does not communicate research.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q20::[MLA.ENG1.RS.01] Prompt: Which source check is most important before publication?
{
~A. Does the source agree with my opinion? #Incorrect. Agreement does not prove source quality.
~B. Is the source title colorful? #Incorrect. Color does not establish reliability.
=C. Is the source reliable, valid, and accurately represented? #Correct. These checks protect source-based communication.
~D. Is the source easy to copy? #Incorrect. Copying is not the goal of research.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q21::[MLA.ENG1.W.03] Prompt: Which sentence avoids overstatement?
{
~A. Digital reminders make every student attend every club. #Incorrect. This is an unsupported absolute claim.
~B. Nobody ever misses announcements again. #Incorrect. This overstates the finding.
=C. The findings suggest that digital reminders may help some students notice club deadlines. #Correct. This uses cautious, accurate language.
~D. Digital reminders solve school communication forever. #Incorrect. This is exaggerated.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q22::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: Which final review question best checks audience fit?
{
=A. Can the intended reader understand the purpose and main findings quickly? #Correct. Audience fit depends on reader understanding.
~B. Did I use the fanciest possible words? #Incorrect. Fancy words may not fit the audience.
~C. Did I hide the main point? #Incorrect. Publication should make the main point clear.
~D. Did I remove all headings? #Incorrect. Headings often help the audience.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q23::[MLA.ENG1.V.01] Prompt: Which word best fits publication-ready review? "The product should be _____ before it is shared."
{
~A. random #Incorrect. Random work is not publication-ready.
=B. accurate #Correct. Accuracy is essential before sharing source-based communication.
~C. hidden #Incorrect. Publication is meant to be shared.
~D. copied #Incorrect. Copying does not make work publication-ready.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q24::[MLA.ENG1.W.03] Prompt: Which sentence should be revised before publication?
{
~A. A survey found that some students used quiet rooms to reduce distractions. #Incorrect. This is accurate and clear.
~B. The findings suggest that tutoring may support confidence. #Incorrect. This is cautious and source-based.
~C. A report explains that online calendars can help students review due dates after class. #Incorrect. This integrates a finding clearly.
=D. The source totally proves the best idea ever. #Correct. This is vague, informal, and exaggerated.
}

::ENG1_U04_L07_Q25::[MLA.ENG1.C.03] Prompt: What does publication-ready source-based communication require?
{
=A. Accurate findings, clear organization, audience fit, and standard English. #Correct. These qualities prepare work for readers.
~B. Random facts and no audience. #Incorrect. Publication needs audience and organization.
~C. Exaggerated claims and hidden sources. #Incorrect. These weaken accuracy and trust.
~D. A private draft with no revision. #Incorrect. Publication-ready work must be revised and shareable.
}
